% Two element constraints on ONE index, which is the scene the M2-T10 consistency oracle
% actually bites on -- and the first place in this tree where it does.
%
% D-0059 measured that the Bounds/Domain tag "does no separating work on anything
% currently shipped", because every Bounds instance then in the tree was a single linear
% inequality, and for one inequality bounds consistency IS domain consistency. It named
% all_different and a product as the places that would change, and `element` is the other
% one. Measured on this model 2026-09-21: with lib/core/prop/element.ml's interior-hole
% removal disabled -- i.e. relabelled Bounds in all but the declaration -- the oracle
% fires, naming `c = 4` and saying a domain-consistent propagator would have removed it.
% That is D-0059's lane 9 going red on purpose, so the tag is now load-bearing.
%
% Run it with BAGUETTE_CONSISTENCY=1; the shared index is what makes the two constraints
% inform each other, which is what gives the oracle more than a handful of tuples.
var 1..4: i :: output_var;
var 0..9: c :: output_var;
var 0..9: d :: output_var;
constraint array_int_element(i, [3, 7, 3, 5], c);
constraint array_int_element(i, [8, 2, 6, 2], d);
constraint int_lin_le([1,1],[c,d],11);
solve satisfy;
